WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.380
The outcry to regulate big tech
companies is getting louder! There are

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:13.500
several questions. One is: Should sites
like Facebook be held accountable for

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.330
their site's content? Yes... by their
customers. Customers know more about the

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.100
good and bad of the content than do
government regulators, and government

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.820
regulators have their own agendas and
biases. So we should proceed cautiously

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.439
before regulating content. Should
government limit big tech's collection and

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:35.640
use of big data? This question should
become moot after a few years, as

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.360
blockchain is likely to give customers
control of their data. Between now and

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.920
then, the best thing to do for customers
is provide them with transparency. Should

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.910
the government break up big tech? That
makes no practical sense. Which of your

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.969
friends do you want kicked off
Facebook? Do you want your Google

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:56.100
searches diverted to Ask.com? Or your
Amazon purchases diverted to Walmart? If

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.570
the government did break up one or more
companies, customer choice would put them

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.890
back together. What's the bottom line? In
fast moving markets, customers and

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.100
competition are the best regulators.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.020
Do
you think big tech should be more

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.150
regulated? Let us know your comments. Also,
let us know what other topics you'd like

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:21.210
for our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,
and be sure to like and subscribe for

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.549
more research and videos from AEI.

